    "title": "Sawyerville-Eoline Tornado - April 27, 2011",
    "url": "https://www.weather.gov/bmx/event_04272011sawyerville",
    "publisher_or_author": "National Weather Service Birmingham, Alabama (NOAA/NWS)",
    "date_published_or_updated": null,
    "source_type": "government (NWS event survey)",
    "triggering_query": "\"2011 Sawyerville–Eoline tornado\" -site:en.wikipedia.org",
    "relevance_to_goal": "Core primary-source survey of the specific April 27, 2011 Sawyerville–Eoline EF3 tornado, 
including official rating, path statistics, injuries/fatalities, and narrative damage description across Greene, Hale, 
and Bibb Counties. Explicitly uses the name “Sawyerville-Eoline Tornado”.",
    "raw_content_or_summary": "Page header indicates NWS Birmingham (Weather Forecast Office) under NOAA.\n\nMain 
heading: “Sawyerville-Eoline Tornado - April 27, 2011”. Within the table: “Sawyerville-Eoline (Greene, Hale and Bibb 
Counties) EF-3 Tornado April 27, 2011”. Key statistics:\n- Rating: EF-3 (Enhanced Fujita Scale)\n- Estimated Maximum 
Wind: 145 mph\n- Injuries/Fatalities: 50 Injuries / 7 Fatalities\n- Damage Path Length: 72.13 miles\n- Maximum Path 
Width: 1760 yards (1 mile)\n- Approximate Start Point/Time: 32.6152 / -88.0543 at 5:30 pm\n- Approximate End Point/Time:
33.1524 / -86.9898 at 6:55 pm\n\nNarrative survey (abridged, but kept faithful):\n- NWS meteorologists surveyed multiple
tracks across Greene, Hale and Bibb Counties and determined damage consistent with a strong tornado.\n- Tornado touched 
down in far southwest Greene County near the Tombigbee River just west of Tishabee on County Road 69 near Head Drive, 
moving northeast across rural Greene County north of Forkland.\n- In Greene County: at least 4 mobile homes, 3 
outbuildings, and 2 churches damaged or destroyed; numerous trees down; two minor injuries. Track started as EF-1 and 
increased to EF-2 while in Greene County.\n- The tornado crossed the Black Warrior River into Hale County west of 
Sawyerville. It crossed AL Route 14 just north of Sawyerville, where it intensified to EF-3 with winds of 145 mph.\n- 
Continuing northeast: crossed CR 18, CR 21, AL Hwy 69 south of Harper Hill, and CR 29 east of Ingram. Extensive 
structural damage in these locations led to at least 40 injuries and 6 fatalities. Numerous mobile homes and 
single-family homes and one church were damaged or destroyed. Thousands of trees were knocked down.\n- The tornado moved
into Talladega National Forest near Ingram, causing significant tree damage up to the Bibb County line.\n- It tracked 
northeastward through the national forest, then moved through Eoline, causing significant structural damage consistent 
with EF-3 rating and 145 mph winds. Numerous mobile homes and single-family homes sustained minor to major damage; a 
dozen mobile homes and single-family homes were destroyed. The Eoline Fire Department and another business were 
destroyed. One fatality occurred in a vehicle near the fire department; at least 10 other injuries noted.\n- The tornado
continued northeast, crossing AL Hwy 5 south of West Blocton, weakening slightly to EF-2 with 125 mph winds. Several 
mobile homes and single-family homes sustained major damage or were destroyed near CR 26. Thousands of trees were downed
along the path. The tornado continued to weaken and lifted just northeast of Marvel.\n- Tornado warnings were in effect 
for Greene, Hale and Bibb Counties from 4:50 pm until 7:45 pm.\n\nThe page also links to radar images and other event 
resources, but the core content is the detailed official event summary above."
  },
