[AIMessage → tool] create_analysis({'text': 'Synthesis for article drafting: The Sawyerville–Eoline tornado can be 
described as an EF3 long-track tornado that occurred on April 27, 2011 across Greene, Hale, and Bibb counties in 
Alabama, during the peak day of the 2011 Super Outbreak. Official NWS Birmingham documentation and the Alabama Tornado 
Database give a path length of about 72.13 miles, maximum width about 1760 yards, estimated peak winds near 145 mph, and
totals of 7 fatalities and around 50 injuries. County-level narratives provide detail: initial EF1–EF2 damage in rural 
Greene County (mobile homes, churches, minor injuries); intensification to EF3 near Sawyerville in Hale County, where 
extensive structural damage led to about 40 injuries and 6 deaths with many homes and one church destroyed; continued 
EF3 damage through the Talladega National Forest and Eoline in Bibb County, destroying several mobile and single-family 
homes, the Eoline Fire Department and a business, causing one vehicular fatality and at least 10 injuries, before 
weakening to EF2 near Alabama Highway 5 south of West Blocton and lifting northeast of Marvel. Secondary outbreak 
overviews (Tornado Talk, Grokipedia, al.com) situate this tornado within a multi-day outbreak that produced more than 
360 confirmed tornadoes and hundreds of deaths, with modest discrepancies in aggregate statistics that should be 
acknowledged but not overstated. No clear sources were found describing detailed emergency response or long-term 
recovery for this specific tornado, so the article should note the absence of such coverage rather than speculate.', 
'query_id': '20260702014934637851_abe7f776'})
