[tools:0ba9c219-3193-6ab4-5e68-0131cbef41c6] [AIMessage → tool] commit_report_chapter({'chapter_id': 2, 'title': 
'Meteorological synopsis', 'content': 'The Sawyerville–Eoline tornado formed within a broader episode of severe 
convective storms on 27 April 2011, when a powerful storm system produced numerous tornadoes across Alabama and 
surrounding states.[1][9] The NWS Birmingham office’s event overview for the "Historic Outbreak of April 27, 2011" 
documents a series of tornadoes in central Alabama on that date, including long-track, strong, and violent tornadoes 
associated with supercell thunderstorms.[9] Within this sequence, the Sawyerville–Eoline tornado is catalogued as an EF3
tornado affecting Greene, Hale, and Bibb Counties, with an extended track and substantial casualty 
figures.[1][2][9]\n\nAccording to the NWS damage survey specific to the event, the tornado touched down in far 
southwestern Greene County near the Tombigbee River just west of Tishabee, then moved northeast across rural portions of
the county north of Forkland.[2] The survey reports that the tornado subsequently crossed the Black Warrior River into 
Hale County, west of Sawyerville, and intensified as it approached and crossed Alabama Route 14 north of Sawyerville, 
reaching EF3 intensity with estimated winds of about 145 mph (233 km/h).[2] As the tornado progressed through Hale 
County, it crossed local roads including County Roads 18, 21, and 29 and Alabama Highway 69, producing extensive 
structural damage and numerous casualties.[2]\n\nContinuing northeastward, the tornado entered the Talladega National 
Forest near the community of Ingram and tracked toward the Bibb County line, where it caused widespread tree damage 
consistent with its strong intensity.[2] In Bibb County, the tornado moved through the Eoline area, where the NWS survey
describes significant structural damage characteristic of EF3 intensity, with winds again estimated near 145 mph.[2] In 
Eoline, numerous mobile homes and single-family residences sustained minor to major damage, a dozen such structures were
destroyed, and community facilities including the Eoline Fire Department and a nearby business were demolished.[2] The 
survey notes that the tornado weakened slightly to EF2 intensity with estimated winds near 125 mph (201 km/h) as it 
crossed Alabama Highway 5 south of West Blocton; several additional mobile homes and homes were heavily damaged or 
destroyed near County Road 26, and thousands of trees were downed along the later portion of the path before the tornado
dissipated just northeast of Marvel.[2]\n\nThe Alabama Tornado Database for 2011 and the NWS event overview both list 
the Sawyerville–Eoline tornado with a total path length of approximately 72.13 mi and a maximum width of about 1,760 yd,
underscoring its status as one of the longer-track tornadoes of that day in central Alabama.[1][2][9] The tabular 
database provides the tornado’s date, time, counties affected, EF rating, path statistics, and casualty totals, while 
the narrative survey adds detailed geographic and intensity information along the track.[1][2]', 'source_ids': 
