[tools:0ba9c219-3193-6ab4-5e68-0131cbef41c6] [assemble_report_draft result] {"status": "ok", "draft_path": 
"outputs/wlc_reports7/_runs/95-2011_Sawyerville–Eoline_tornado/.report_draft.md", "draft": "# 2011 Sawyerville–Eoline 
tornado\n\n## Overview\n\nThe 2011 Sawyerville–Eoline tornado was a long-track, violent tornado that struck portions of 
Greene, Hale, and Bibb Counties in west-central Alabama on 27 April 2011, during the historic late-April 2011 tornado 
outbreak in the southeastern United States. According to official National Weather Service (NWS) documentation, the 
tornado was rated EF3 on the Enhanced Fujita scale, with a surveyed damage path length of 72.13 mi (116.1 km) and a 
maximum path width of 1,760 yd (1.0 mi). The event caused severe damage to homes, businesses, community facilities, and 
forested areas along its route and resulted in seven fatalities and fifty injuries. [1][2][9]\n\nThe tornado is named 
for the communities of Sawyerville in Hale County and Eoline in Bibb County, which experienced some of the most intense 
damage, including destruction of residential structures and the local fire department in Eoline. The NWS Birmingham 
office lists the Sawyerville–Eoline tornado among the significant tornadoes of 27 April 2011, a day on which numerous 
strong and violent tornadoes affected Alabama. The Alabama Tornado Database for 2011 includes a distinct entry for this 
tornado, documenting its EF3 rating, multi-county track through Greene, Hale, and Bibb Counties, and associated 
casualties. [1][2][9]\n\n## Meteorological synopsis\n\nThe Sawyerville–Eoline tornado formed within a broader episode of
severe convective storms on 27 April 2011, when a powerful storm system produced numerous tornadoes across Alabama and 
surrounding states. The NWS Birmingham office’s event overview for the \"Historic Outbreak of April 27, 2011\" documents
a series of tornadoes in central Alabama on that date, including long-track, strong, and violent tornadoes associated 
with supercell thunderstorms. Within this sequence, the Sawyerville–Eoline tornado is catalogued as an EF3 tornado 
affecting Greene, Hale, and Bibb Counties, with an extended track and substantial casualty figures. 
[1][2][9]\n\nAccording to the NWS damage survey specific to the event, the tornado touched down in far southwestern 
Greene County near the Tombigbee River just west of Tishabee, then moved northeast across rural portions of the county 
north of Forkland. The survey reports that the tornado subsequently crossed the Black Warrior River into Hale County, 
west of Sawyerville, and intensified as it approached and crossed Alabama Route 14 north of Sawyerville, reaching EF3 
intensity with estimated winds of about 145 mph (233 km/h). As the tornado progressed through Hale County, it crossed 
local roads including County Roads 18, 21, and 29 and Alabama Highway 69, producing extensive structural damage and 
numerous casualties. [1][2][9]\n\nContinuing northeastward, the tornado entered the Talladega National Forest near the 
community of Ingram and tracked toward the Bibb County line, where it caused widespread tree damage consistent with its 
strong intensity. In Bibb County, the tornado moved through the Eoline area, where the NWS survey describes significant 
structural damage characteristic of EF3 intensity, with winds again estimated near 145 mph. In Eoline, numerous mobile 
homes and single-family residences sustained minor to major damage, a dozen such structures were destroyed, and 
community facilities including the Eoline Fire Department and a nearby business were demolished. The survey notes that 
the tornado weakened slightly to EF2 intensity with estimated winds near 125 mph (201 km/h) as it crossed Alabama 
Highway 5 south of West Blocton; several additional mobile homes and homes were heavily damaged or destroyed near County
Road 26, and thousands of trees were downed along the later portion of the path before the tornado dissipated just 
northeast of Marvel. [1][2][9]\n\nThe Alabama Tornado Database for 2011 and the NWS event overview both list the 
Sawyerville–Eoline tornado with a total path length of approximately 72.13 mi and a maximum width of about 1,760 yd, 
underscoring its status as one of the longer-track tornadoes of that day in central Alabama. The tabular database 
provides the tornado’s date, time, counties affected, EF rating, path statistics, and casualty totals, while the 
narrative survey adds detailed geographic and intensity information along the track. [1][2][9]\n\n## Impact and 
damage\n\nThe Sawyerville–Eoline tornado produced severe and varied impacts along its multi-county path, including 
extensive structural and vegetation damage and multiple casualties. The NWS Birmingham damage survey and the Alabama 
Tornado Database both attribute seven fatalities and fifty injuries to this tornado, distributed among Greene, Hale, and
Bibb Counties. The NWS overview of the \"Historic Outbreak of April 27, 2011\" also lists these casualty figures for the
event in its tabular summary of the day’s tornadoes. [1][2][9]\n\nIn Greene County, the tornado initially struck rural 
areas near the Tombigbee River just west of Tishabee, moving northeast across sparsely populated terrain north of 
Forkland. The survey notes that at least four mobile homes, three outbuildings, and two churches were damaged or 
destroyed in this segment of the track, and two minor injuries were reported. Although the early portion of the path 
affected fewer structures than later segments, the damage to small community facilities and residences in Greene County 
illustrates the breadth of impacts even at lower population density. [1][2][9]\n\nUpon crossing the Black Warrior River 
into Hale County, west of Sawyerville, the tornado encountered more developed areas and intensified as it crossed 
Alabama Route 14 north of Sawyerville, reaching EF3 strength with estimated winds near 145 mph. In this region, the 
tornado passed over and near local roads including County Roads 18, 21, and 29 and Alabama Highway 69 south of Harper 
Hill, generating extensive structural damage. The NWS survey reports that at least forty injuries and six fatalities 
occurred in Hale County as homes and other buildings were destroyed or heavily damaged along this part of the track. 
Thousands of trees were also uprooted or snapped, reflecting the strength and breadth of the circulation. 
[1][2][9]\n\nThe tornado’s impacts continued into Bibb County, where it moved through the Talladega National Forest near
Ingram and then into the Eoline area. In forested sections, the survey describes significant tree damage, while in 
Eoline the tornado again produced EF3-level destruction, with winds estimated near 145 mph. Numerous mobile homes and 
single-family houses in Eoline sustained damage ranging from minor to major, and about a dozen such structures were 
destroyed outright. Community infrastructure in Eoline was also affected: the Eoline Fire Department building and 
another nearby business were destroyed when the tornado passed through the area. The survey documents one fatality in a 
vehicle near the fire department and at least ten other injuries in Bibb County, underscoring the localized severity of 
the event. [1][2][9]\n\nFarther along its path, as the tornado crossed Alabama Highway 5 south of West Blocton, it 
weakened to EF2 intensity with estimated winds near 125 mph but continued to inflict major damage on several mobile 
homes and single-family residences near County Road 26. Thousands of additional trees were downed along the later 
portion of the track before the tornado lifted just northeast of Marvel, at the northeastern end of its surveyed path. 
Across all three counties, the combination of residential, commercial, community-facility, and forest damage, together 
with the documented casualty figures, mark the Sawyerville–Eoline tornado as one of the more destructive EF3 events of 
the April 27 outbreak in central Alabama. The available NWS and database sources do not provide a specific monetary 
estimate of economic losses for this tornado, so the financial impact can only be inferred from the documented extent of
structural and vegetation damage. [1][2][9]\n\n## Aftermath and response\n\nPublished National Weather Service 
documentation and the Alabama Tornado Database primarily focus on the meteorological characteristics and direct impacts 
of the Sawyerville–Eoline tornado, and they do not provide detailed narrative accounts of the emergency response or 
long-term recovery specific to this event. Academic and public-health analyses of the broader April 27, 2011 Alabama 
tornado outbreak, which include this tornado among many others, describe coordinated emergency medical response and 
public-safety actions across the state but generally treat the Sawyerville–Eoline tornado as part of the aggregate of 
storms rather than as a separate case study. In these sources, local emergency services, hospitals, and public-health 
agencies are reported to have managed large numbers of injured persons and fatalities from multiple tornadoes on 27 
April, including those in Greene, Hale, and Bibb Counties. [1][2][9][11]\n\nBecause the available NWS and database 
materials emphasize meteorological surveys and damage statistics and the statewide mortality and outbreak studies 
synthesize impacts across many tornadoes, specific information on community-level rebuilding efforts, assistance 
programs, or memorials dedicated solely to the Sawyerville–Eoline tornado is not documented in these sources. As a 
result, while it is clear from official records that emergency responders, medical personnel, and public agencies were 
engaged in managing the consequences of this tornado along with others in the outbreak, detailed accounts of the 
response and recovery in the individual communities of Sawyerville, Eoline, and nearby rural areas remain limited in the
available non-Wikipedia documentation. [1][2][9][11]\n\n## Context within the 2011 Super Outbreak and data 
conflicts\n\nThe Sawyerville–Eoline tornado occurred during the late-April 2011 tornado outbreak, often referred to as 
the 2011 Super Outbreak, which affected large portions of the central and eastern United States. Outbreak overviews from
Grokipedia and Tornado Talk describe a multi-day sequence of severe thunderstorms and tornadoes from 25 to 28 April 
2011, with 27 April representing the single most active and deadly day of the event. Both sources identify Alabama as 
one of the states hardest hit by strong and violent tornadoes on 27 April, with numerous long-track tornadoes causing 
widespread destruction and hundreds of fatalities. Within Alabama, the NWS Birmingham \"Historic Outbreak of April 27, 
2011\" page similarly documents many tornadoes of EF2 intensity or greater, including the Sawyerville–Eoline EF3 tornado
in Greene, Hale, and Bibb Counties. [6][8][9][1][2]\n\nAlthough Grokipedia and Tornado Talk agree on the exceptional 
severity of the 2011 Super Outbreak, their summary statistics differ slightly. Grokipedia reports that the outbreak 
produced about 362 tornadoes in total, whereas the Tornado Talk overview gives a figure of about 368 tornadoes, 
reflecting differences in data sources or counting methodology. For 27 April specifically, Grokipedia cites 
approximately 199 tornadoes, while Tornado Talk estimates about 224 tornadoes for that day. The two sources also report 
slightly different overall fatality totals, with Grokipedia giving about 321 deaths associated with the outbreak and 
Tornado Talk listing about 324 fatalities. Despite these minor discrepancies, both accounts consistently portray the 
outbreak as one of the most significant tornado events in U.S. history and emphasize the major role of Alabama 
tornadoes, including the Sawyerville–Eoline storm, in contributing to the high casualty and damage figures. 
[6][8][9][1][2]\n\nThe NWS Birmingham outbreak summary for 27 April 2011 provides a more localized operational 
perspective, listing the Sawyerville–Eoline tornado alongside other significant Alabama tornadoes in a tabular format 
that includes time, affected counties, EF rating, path length, maximum width, and casualty statistics. This official 
record aligns with the Alabama Tornado Database for 2011 in identifying the Sawyerville–Eoline tornado as an EF3 event 
with a long track across three counties, seven fatalities, and fifty injuries, situating it among the more destructive 
but not highest-rated tornadoes (which included several EF4 and EF5 tornadoes) during the outbreak in central Alabama. 
[6][8][9][1][2]\n\n## References\n\n[1] Alabama Tornado Database 2011 https://www.weather.gov/bmx/tornadodb_2011\n\n[2] 
Sawyerville-Eoline Tornado - April 27, 2011 https://www.weather.gov/bmx/event_04272011sawyerville\n\n[6] 2011 Super 
Outbreak — Grokipedia https://grokipedia.com/page/2011_Super_Outbreak\n\n[8] Overview of the April 25-28, 2011 Super 
Outbreak – Tornado Talk https://www.tornadotalk.com/overview-of-the-april-25-28-2011-super-outbreak\n\n[9] Historic 
Outbreak of April 27, 2011 https://www.weather.gov/bmx/event_04272011\n\n[11] Mortality From a Tornado Outbreak, 
Alabama, April 27, 2011 https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4007884\n", "issues": [], "revision_requested": false}
