[AIMessage] 2011 Sawyerville–Eoline tornado

The 2011 Sawyerville–Eoline tornado was a long-track, high-end EF3 tornado that struck Greene, Hale, and Bibb counties 
in central Alabama on April 27, 2011, during the peak day of the 2011 Super Outbreak of tornadoes across the 
southeastern United States.[1][2][3] According to the National Weather Service (NWS) office in Birmingham, Alabama, the 
tornado had an estimated maximum wind speed of about 145 mph, a damage path length of approximately 72.13 miles, and a 
maximum path width of around 1,760 yards (roughly 1 mile).[1][2] The tornado caused extensive destruction in rural 
communities near Sawyerville in Hale County and near Eoline in Bibb County, resulting in about 50 injuries and 7 
fatalities.[1][2][3] It was one of several long-track tornadoes that affected Alabama on April 27 and is recognized in 
both official and secondary accounts as a distinct event within the broader outbreak.[1][2][3][4]

### Background and meteorological setting

The Sawyerville–Eoline tornado occurred during the multi-day severe weather episode of April 25–28, 2011, commonly 
referred to as the 2011 Super Outbreak.[4][5] Secondary summaries describe this outbreak as one of the largest on record
in the United States, producing on the order of 362–368 confirmed tornadoes across 21 states from Texas to New York, 
including 4 EF5, 11 EF4, and 21 EF3 tornadoes.[4][5] Fatalities across the outbreak have been reported in the range of 
about 321–324, with more than 3,100 injuries and estimated economic losses of roughly US$12 billion.[4][5]

Within Alabama, the April 27 portion of the outbreak was especially intense. A retrospective article from al.com reports
that the state experienced about 62 tornadoes over roughly 18 hours on that day, with more than 240 deaths, many 
injuries, and combined tornado paths exceeding 1,200 miles.[3] Other secondary sources summarizing the regional outbreak
report between approximately 199 and 224 tornadoes in a 24‑hour period on April 27, causing around 316 fatalities across
Alabama, Mississippi, Tennessee, Georgia, and Virginia.[4][5] These figures illustrate the scale of the larger event but
differ slightly by source and counting method.

The broader outbreak was associated with a deep upper-level trough, strong wind shear, and very moist, unstable air 
moving northward from the Gulf of Mexico, which together favored the development of numerous strong and violent 
tornadoes.[4][5] The Sawyerville–Eoline tornado formed within this environmental context as one of several long‑track 
storms that moved across central Alabama on the afternoon and early evening of April 27.[1][3][4]

### Formation and track

The Sawyerville–Eoline tornado is documented by the NWS Birmingham office as “Sawyerville‑Eoline (Greene, Hale and Bibb 
Counties) EF‑3 Tornado April 27, 2011.”[1] The official NWS event summary and associated statistics table indicate that 
the tornado touched down in far southwestern Greene County near the Tombigbee River, just west of the community of 
Tishabee, in the vicinity of County Road 69 near Head Drive.[1][2] The approximate starting coordinates are listed as 
32.6152° N, 88.0543° W, with an initial time near 5:30 pm Central Daylight Time (CDT).[2]

From its point of origin, the tornado moved generally northeast across rural Greene County north of Forkland.[1][2] It 
then crossed the Black Warrior River and entered Hale County west of Sawyerville.[1][3] The NWS narrative states that 
the tornado crossed Alabama Route 14 just north of Sawyerville, where it intensified to EF3 strength with estimated 
winds of about 145 mph.[1] After crossing Route 14, it continued northeast, eventually entering the Talladega National 
Forest and crossing into Bibb County.[1]

In Bibb County, the tornado passed through the area around Eoline.[1] The event summary indicates that the tornado 
maintained EF3 intensity in this segment, producing significant structural damage consistent with the estimated 145 mph 
winds.[1] Further along its path, the tornado crossed Alabama Highway 5 south of West Blocton, at which point it 
weakened to EF2 intensity with estimated winds of around 125 mph.[1] The approximate ending coordinates are given as 
33.1524° N, 86.9898° W, with an end time near 6:55 pm CDT.[2] The tornado continued to weaken and was reported to have 
lifted just northeast of the community of Marvel in Bibb County.[1]

The NWS statistics for the event list a damage path length of 72.13 miles and a maximum width of 1,760 yards.[1][2] The 
NWS summary also notes that tornado warnings were in effect for Greene, Hale, and Bibb counties from about 4:50 pm to 
7:45 pm on April 27, overlapping the time during which this tornado was on the ground.[1]

### Intensity and rating

The Sawyerville–Eoline tornado was rated EF3 on the Enhanced Fujita (EF) scale by the NWS Birmingham office.[1] The 
rating is based on observed damage along the track and is accompanied by an estimated maximum wind speed of about 145 
mph.[1][2] The official NWS statistics table explicitly lists the rating as “EF‑3 (Enhanced Fujita Scale)” and the 
“Estimated Maximum Wind” as 145 mph.[2]

Along its path, the NWS narrative describes variations in intensity. In Greene County, the track is described as 
starting at EF1 strength and increasing to EF2 while the tornado remained within that county.[1] The intensification to 
EF3 is identified in Hale County, where the tornado crossed Alabama Route 14 just north of Sawyerville.[1] The tornado 
remained at EF3 strength as it moved through Eoline in Bibb County, before weakening to EF2 as it crossed Alabama 
Highway 5 south of West Blocton and then gradually dissipated northeast of Marvel.[1]

### Impact

#### Overall casualties and damage

The combined statistics for the Sawyerville–Eoline tornado indicate that it caused approximately 50 injuries and 7 
fatalities.[1][2][3] An NWS summary table lists “50 Injuries / 7 Fatalities” for this event, along with the path length 
and maximum width figures.[2] An al.com retrospective on the April 27 Alabama tornadoes similarly lists the event among 
long-track tornadoes with the entry “72.13 miles: EF‑3, Sawyerville‑Eoline. Greene, Hale and Bibb counties, 7 deaths, 50
injuries.”[3] These numbers are consistent across the official and secondary sources cited.

The NWS event narrative describes widespread damage to mobile homes, single‑family residences, churches, and other 
structures along the path, as well as extensive tree damage.[1] The damage was concentrated in rural areas of Greene, 
Hale, and Bibb counties, with particularly severe impacts near Sawyerville and Eoline.[1][3] No specific estimate of 
economic losses attributable solely to this tornado was found in the sources used; available outbreak‑wide summaries 
report economic losses on the order of US$12 billion for the 2011 Super Outbreak as a whole, but do not break down this 
figure to individual tornadoes.[4][5]

#### Greene County

In Greene County, the NWS narrative describes the tornado as initially producing EF1 damage, then intensifying to 
EF2.[1] According to the event summary, at least four mobile homes, three outbuildings, and two churches were damaged or
destroyed in this portion of the path, and numerous trees were downed.[1] The NWS notes that two minor injuries occurred
in Greene County.[1] These impacts were concentrated in rural areas north of Forkland, following the tornado’s approach 
from near the Tombigbee River west of Tishabee.[1][2]

#### Hale County (Sawyerville area)

After crossing the Black Warrior River into Hale County west of Sawyerville, the tornado intensified to EF3 strength.[1]
The NWS event narrative reports that the tornado crossed Alabama Route 14 just north of Sawyerville, where the EF3 
rating and estimated 145 mph winds are applied.[1] In this area, the report describes “extensive structural damage” 
leading to at least 40 injuries and 6 fatalities.[1]

The NWS summary indicates that numerous mobile homes and single‑family homes, as well as at least one church, were 
damaged or destroyed in this segment, and thousands of trees were knocked down.[1] The combination of structural damage 
and falling trees is cited as contributing to the observed casualties.[1] These figures account for the majority of the 
injuries and fatalities attributed to the tornado, according to the county‑level breakdown provided in the NWS narrative
and the aggregate statistics.[1][2][3]

#### Bibb County (Eoline and Marvel area)

In Bibb County, the tornado maintained EF3 intensity as it passed through Eoline.[1] The NWS narrative reports that 
numerous mobile homes and single‑family homes in the area sustained minor to major damage, with about a dozen such homes
described as destroyed.[1] The Eoline Fire Department building and another business were also reported as destroyed.[1]

The NWS notes that one fatality occurred in a vehicle near the fire department and that at least 10 other injuries were 
recorded in this segment of the path.[1] After traveling through Eoline, the tornado continued northeast through wooded 
and rural areas, crossing Alabama Highway 5 south of West Blocton.[1] In this area, the tornado weakened to EF2 
strength, with estimated winds around 125 mph, and then continued to weaken before lifting just northeast of 
Marvel.[1][2]

These impacts in Bibb County contributed one fatality and a portion of the total injuries associated with the event, 
complementing the higher casualty figures reported for the Hale County segment.[1][2][3]

### Place in the 2011 Super Outbreak

Although the sources consulted focus primarily on the local impacts and meteorological details of the Sawyerville–Eoline
tornado, several secondary accounts situate it within the broader 2011 Super Outbreak.[3][4][5][6] The Tornado Talk 
website’s overview of the April 25–28 outbreak lists aggregate statistics for the event, including 368 confirmed 
tornadoes, 4 EF5, 11 EF4, 21 EF3 tornadoes, 224 tornadoes occurring on April 27, and an estimated 324 fatalities, more 
than 3,100 injuries, and roughly US$12 billion in damage.[5] A separate reference at Grokipedia describes the outbreak 
as involving 362 confirmed tornadoes across 21 states and about 321 deaths, with April 27 identified as the peak day, 
during which approximately 199 tornadoes and 316 fatalities occurred.[4] These summaries emphasize that the outbreak as 
a whole combined very large numbers of tornadoes, long path lengths, and substantial casualties.

Within Alabama, al.com’s retrospective article highlights several long‑track tornadoes that occurred on April 27, 
listing them in a section on “long‑track tornadoes.”[3] In this list, the Sawyerville–Eoline tornado is identified 
alongside other long‑track events, such as those that impacted different regions of the state, suggesting that it was 
considered one of the notable long‑track EF3 tornadoes of the day.[3] Al.com reports that Alabama’s tornadoes on April 
27 collectively produced more than 240 deaths and over 1,200 miles of tornado tracks.[3]

Additional context is provided by a documentary‑style video on the 2011 Super Outbreak produced for a YouTube channel 
focused on weather events.[6] The video includes a segment labelled “Sawyerville‑Eoline, AL EF3” in its chapter list, 
indicating that the tornado is discussed individually within the broader narrative of significant storms from the 
outbreak.[6] The inclusion of the tornado as a separate chapter in the video, along with its listing in written outbreak
summaries and the existence of dedicated NWS documentation, reflects that the Sawyerville–Eoline tornado is treated as a
distinct case within the larger regional event.[1][3][5][6]

### Documentation and coverage

The principal technical documentation for the Sawyerville–Eoline tornado is the official event summary prepared by the 
NWS Birmingham office, which provides the EF rating, estimated maximum wind speed, path length, maximum width, 
approximate start and end locations and times, county‑level damage narratives, and casualty statistics.[1][2] The same 
office maintains an Alabama Tornado Database for 2011, organized as a table listing each tornado with fields for date, 
time (CST/CDT), county, damage scale (EF rating), path length in miles, maximum width in yards, fatalities, injuries, 
and a location/damage description.[2][7] The database includes multiple events from April 27, 2011, but the 
Sawyerville–Eoline tornado is singled out in the separate event summary page.[1][2]

Secondary sources supplement the official documentation by compiling outbreak‑wide statistics and retrospective 
narratives. Al.com’s article provides state‑level statistics for Alabama’s April 27 tornadoes and lists the 
Sawyerville–Eoline tornado among notable long‑track events.[3] Tornado Talk’s overview of the outbreak and a related 
Facebook post acknowledge the tornado explicitly as “Sawyerville‑Eoline, AL EF3 Tornado – April 27, 2011,” and note that
a dedicated summary exists on their site.[5][8] Grokipedia presents an independent synopsis of the outbreak’s scale and 
meteorological setting.[4] A Montgomery Advertiser tornado archive entry describes a tornado crossing the Black Warrior 
River into Hale County west of Sawyerville and moving through Eoline, causing significant structural damage, in a manner
consistent with the path described in NWS documentation.[9] The YouTube documentary video on the 2011 Super Outbreak 
includes a chapter on the Sawyerville–Eoline EF3 tornado, placing it alongside other major events from the outbreak.[6]

None of the sources examined provide detailed accounts of emergency response operations, disaster declarations specific 
to this tornado, or long‑term recovery and commemoration activities in the affected communities. The available 
documentation focuses on meteorological characteristics, structural damage, and casualty statistics. In the absence of 
such information, any discussion of response and recovery for the Sawyerville–Eoline event must be limited to noting 
this evidentiary gap rather than attempting to reconstruct those aspects without sources.[1][3][4][5]

